C = [
("h1", "7. Malachi: The Priests and the Pleasant Offering"),
("p", "Claim: Malachi is the last of the prophets, and he is the one who complains most that the offerings are poor. His charge against the priests is that they bring the blind, the lame and the sick to an altar the LORD calls his own. He asks for a better offering, not for none. He ends with a promise that the sons of Levi will be purified so that they may offer an offering in righteousness, and that the offering of Judah and Jerusalem shall be pleasant to the LORD as in the days of old. If any prophet were going to say that the order of sacrifice had come to an end, it would be here, and it is the prophet who says the opposite."),
("sub", "7.1 O priests, that despise my name"),
("p", "The charge opens with a question about honour. “A son honoureth his father, and a servant his master: if then I be a father, where is mine honour? and if I be a master, where is my fear? saith the LORD of hosts unto you, O priests, that despise my name” (Malachi 1:6). The audience is the priests, and the charge is contempt for the name. The priests reply, “Wherein have we despised thy name?” The word of the LORD is that they offer “polluted bread upon mine altar” (Malachi 1:7). The altar is “mine altar.” The table is “the table of the LORD,” which the priests call contemptible. The house, the altar and the table are the LORD’s, and the complaint is about what the priests put on them."),
("sub", "7.2 The blind, the lame and the sick"),
("p", "The offence is stated in detail. “And if ye offer the blind for sacrifice, is it not evil? and if ye offer the lame and sick, is it not evil? offer it now unto thy governor; will he be pleased with thee, or accept thy person? saith the LORD of hosts” (Malachi 1:8). The comparison is with a governor. A man would not bring a blind animal to the governor of the province and expect to be received. The priests bring to the altar what they would not bring to a man. The LORD is a great King (Malachi 1:14). The weight of the charge is that the offering is treated as a thing of no account, to be filled with whatever is to hand."),
("p", "Verse 13 supplies the attitude. “Ye said also, Behold, what a weariness is it! and ye have snuffed at it, saith the LORD of hosts; and ye brought that which was torn, and the lame, and the sick; thus ye brought an offering: should I accept this of your hand? saith the LORD” (Malachi 1:13). The priests find the service a weariness and sniff at it. They bring what is torn, lame and sick. The LORD asks whether he should accept it. Verse 14 turns to the worshiper: “But cursed be the deceiver, which hath in his flock a male, and voweth, and sacrificeth unto the LORD a corrupt thing: for I am a great King, saith the LORD of hosts, and my name is dreadful among the heathen” (Malachi 1:14). A man who has a male in his flock, vows it, and then offers a corrupt thing instead is a deceiver. The vow is the language of Psalm 50:14, and the curse falls on the man who does not pay it."),
("sub", "7.3 Who would shut the doors?"),
("p", "The strongest sentence is in verse 10. “Who is there even among you that would shut the doors for nought? neither do ye kindle fire on mine altar for nought. I have no pleasure in you, saith the LORD of hosts, neither will I accept an offering at your hand” (Malachi 1:10). The LORD wishes that someone among the priests would shut the doors of the house. He says they do not kindle fire on his altar for nothing: they take a living by it. The sentence does not say the doors should be shut for ever. It says that offerings of this kind, from these priests, would be better not offered, and it is an appeal to the priests to stop."),
("p", "The next verse is the most difficult in the book for any reader. “For from the rising of the sun even unto the going down of the same my name shall be great among the Gentiles; and in every place incense shall be offered unto my name, and a pure offering: for my name shall be great among the heathen, saith the LORD of hosts” (Malachi 1:11). The volume reports the verse as written and draws three small conclusions. The sentence is a statement of what shall be, as the King James Version puts it, in the future. It contrasts a pure offering with the polluted bread of verse 7. And it speaks of the LORD’s name among the Gentiles in language like that of Isaiah 66:19–21. The volume does not say when this is, and it does not read the verse as a description of the Body of Christ. It stands in Israel’s prophetic line, with the hope of a purified offering, and says what the LORD says: his name will be great among the nations, and a pure offering will be given."),
("sub", "7.4 The covenant of Levi"),
("p", "Chapter 2 repeats the charge to the priests and sets it in the covenant. “And now, O ye priests, this commandment is for you. If ye will not hear, and if ye will not lay it to heart, to give glory unto my name, saith the LORD of hosts, I will even send a curse upon you, and I will curse your blessings” (Malachi 2:1, 2). The sentence is severe: “I will corrupt your seed, and spread dung upon your faces, even the dung of your solemn feasts” (Malachi 2:3). The solemn feasts are named as the priests’ own, and the sentence turns them to shame."),
("p", "The covenant explains why the offence is so great. “My covenant was with him of life and peace; and I gave them to him for the fear wherewith he feared me, and was afraid before my name. The law of truth was in his mouth, and iniquity was not found in his lips: he walked with me in peace and equity, and did turn many away from iniquity” (Malachi 2:5, 6). The priest at his best is a teacher: “the priest’s lips should keep knowledge, and they should seek the law at his mouth: for he is the messenger of the LORD of hosts” (Malachi 2:7). The priests of Malachi’s day have departed out of the way, caused many to stumble at the law, and corrupted the covenant of Levi (Malachi 2:8). The charge is not that Levi’s office is false. It is that the sons of Levi have fallen from it, and the covenant is the standard by which they are measured."),
("sub", "7.5 The refiner, and an offering in righteousness"),
("p", "The third chapter begins with the coming of a messenger and the LORD to his temple; Volume 4 reads those two messengers and the verses around them. The verses that follow belong to this volume. The coming is a day of testing: “But who may abide the day of his coming? and who shall stand when he appeareth? for he is like a refiner’s fire, and like fullers’ soap” (Malachi 3:2). Then the purpose is stated. “And he shall sit as a refiner and purifier of silver: and he shall purify the sons of Levi, and purge them as gold and silver, that they may offer unto the LORD an offering in righteousness. Then shall the offering of Judah and Jerusalem be pleasant unto the LORD, as in the days of old, and as in former years” (Malachi 3:3, 4)."),
("p", "Three things are plain in the two verses. The object of the refining is the sons of Levi, the priests whose offerings were polluted. The purpose of the refining is “that they may offer unto the LORD an offering in righteousness.” And the result is that the offering of Judah and Jerusalem shall be “pleasant unto the LORD, as in the days of old, and as in former years.” The prophet who charged the priests with offering the blind and the lame ends by promising the offering that pleases. He does not promise an end to the order. He promises its purification. The verse that follows lists the sins that bring him near to judgment: sorcerers, adulterers, false swearers, those who oppress the hireling, the widow, the fatherless and the stranger (Malachi 3:5). The list is conduct. The sins of verse 5 are the sins of the earlier prophets."),
("p", "Malachi 3:6 gives the ground on which the sons of Jacob are not consumed: “For I am the LORD, I change not; therefore ye sons of Jacob are not consumed.” Volume 1 read that verse among the sworn words. It is cited here only to mark that Malachi’s hope for the offering and his word about the LORD’s constancy stand together in the same chapter."),
("p", "The book ends with an instruction. “Remember ye the law of Moses my servant, which I commanded unto him in Horeb for all Israel, with the statutes and judgments” (Malachi 4:4). The last charge of the prophets is to remember the law given at Horeb, with its statutes and judgments. Volume 4 reads the verse that follows. In this volume it is enough to note what the final prophet asks: not that Israel leave the Law behind, but that it remember it."),
("ctbl", "Malachi in five passages.", [
    ("Passage", "What the LORD says against it", "What stands"),
    ("Malachi 1:6, 7", "The priests despise his name and offer polluted bread; the table is called contemptible", "“Mine altar”; “the table of the LORD”"),
    ("Malachi 1:8, 13, 14", "The blind, the lame, the sick and the torn are offered; the deceiver who vows a male and offers a corrupt thing is cursed", "The offering is asked; it must be fit for a great King"),
    ("Malachi 1:10", "Better that someone shut the doors than that fire be kindled on the altar for nought", "An appeal to the priests; no sentence of abolition"),
    ("Malachi 2:1–9", "The priests have departed out of the way and corrupted the covenant of Levi", "The covenant with Levi was of life and peace; the priest is the messenger of the LORD of hosts (2:7)"),
    ("Malachi 3:3, 4", "None against", "The sons of Levi purified, offering an offering in righteousness; the offering of Judah and Jerusalem pleasant as in the days of old"),
  ], [0.2, 0.42, 0.38]),
("sub", "7.6 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Malachi’s whole complaint is that the offerings are poor; he asks for a fit offering, and he says that a priest’s lips should keep knowledge.",
    "The altar, the table and the doors belong to the LORD in the language of the book: “mine altar,” “the table of the LORD.”",
    "Malachi 1:10 wishes that the doors were shut rather than that the fire be kindled for nought, and it is an appeal to the priests and not a sentence of abolition.",
    "Malachi 1:11 is reported as written, a promise that his name will be great among the Gentiles and a pure offering given, and it is placed in Israel’s prophetic line without a date.",
    "Malachi 3:3, 4 promises the sons of Levi purified and the offering of Judah and Jerusalem pleasant as in the days of old; the last prophet closes by telling Israel to remember the law of Moses.",
  ]),
]
